;;; examples/sha256sum/sha256sum_elf.shard — package the PROVEN sha256sum machine
;;; module (examples/sha256sum/sha256sum_x86_out.shard) as a real static Linux ELF
;;; (docs/IMP.md I2e-4c-4, path A: the one-time on-silicon confirmation).
;;; UNTRUSTED plumbing, no proofs — a wrong header/stub byte fails LOUDLY
;;; at the loader or the NIST diff, never in a proof.
;;;
;;; THE ARGV DIVERGENCE: shs_main (image index 6) takes NO argv — the
;;; artifact claims enter it with an all-zero MkRegs. So the `_start` stub
;;; ZEROES all 15 model registers and `call`s the entry; it does NOT
;;; marshal argv (the one structural difference from addw's world-bin glue).
;;; shs_main self-exits through the in-image exit shim, so there is no exit
;;; tail — a single `ud2` trap fence sits after the `call` (the §44 never-
;;; returns fence), and the process's observable behaviour is the entry's
;;; own trace (read / write / exit through the in-image shims).
;;;
;;; THE WINDOW, AND WHY THIS FILE SHRANK. The module window is the weld's
;;; [65536, 131072) — the base-65536 shift (docs/STREAM.md §7.9 M5 slice 1)
;;; moved it there, and every address literal in
;;; examples/sha256sum/sha256sum_x86_out.shard now carries that base: the read
;;; buffer and the hex digest both sit at 65536, and the module term the claims
;;; quantify over is `(MkXModule … 65536 131072)`. 65536 is exactly stock
;;; vm.mmap_min_addr, so the mapping needs NO privilege — the page-0 hack this
;;; file used to require (`setcap cap_sys_rawio+ep`, the
;;; `sysctl vm.mmap_min_addr=0` fallback, and the build-onto-a-non-nosuid-FS
;;; dance) is GONE, and with it the hand-rolled ELF container that existed only
;;; to place a PT_LOAD at vaddr 0.
;;;
;;; What replaced it is models/x86/elf.shard's `enc_winelf` (the #65 window
;;; rung, X86.md §52) — the same two segments, the same no-argv all-zero glue,
;;; the same `ud2` fence, the same ascending-vaddr phdr order, with the window
;;; base a PARAMETER. This file's whole job is now naming its arguments: the
;;; image, the entry offset, and the window the module itself declares.
;;;
;;; ⚠ THE STALENESS THIS REPAIRS (2026-08-01). Between the base-65536 shift and
;;; this change the module moved but the container here did not, so this
;;; emitter produced an ELF whose RW PT_LOAD sat at vaddr 0 while every address
;;; in the image pointed at 65536. It still CHECKED GREEN — the file carries no
;;; claims, so nothing in the corpus could catch it — and the binary SIGSEGV'd
;;; on the first vector. Recorded here and not only in the ledger, because the
;;; shape recurs: a hand-copied layout constant that duplicates a model
;;; constant is a landmine, and the repair is to stop duplicating it. The
;;; window below is the same pair the claims carry.
;;;
;;; PURE BY THE BIN-BOUNDARY LAW: this file is a HOST TOOL over the machine
;;; module — it must NOT drag a World main into the closure, because the
;;; closure already carries the `(bin sha256sum_oneshot … (externs nw_read
;;; nw_write nw_exit))` form (via sha256sum_x86_out → sha256sum_src) and the boundary
;;; law demands the closure's declared-extern surface EQUAL the bin's list.
;;; So the ELF bytes are a nullary fn `shs_elf`; the World glue that writes
;;; them to disk lives in the run-only examples/sha256sum/sha256sum_write.shard.

(import "../../kernel/stdlib.shard")
(import "../../models/x86/elf.shard")
(import "sha256sum_x86_out.shard")
(use (:: models x86 elf enc_winelf))
(use (:: examples sha256sum sha256sum_x86_out shs_elf_image))
(use (:: examples sha256sum sha256sum_x86_out shs_elf_offsets))

(fn nth ((xs (List Int)) (i Int)) Int
  (measure (struct xs))
  (match xs
    (Nil 0)
    ((Cons h t) (if (int_eq i 0) h (nth t (- i 1))))))

;; shs_main = image index 6, the index every entry claim hands to xrun_w
(fn entry_off () Int (nth (shs_elf_offsets) 6))

;; the whole static ELF, as raw bytes (each in [0,256)). The window arguments
;; are the module's own declared bounds — (MkXModule … 65536 131072), i.e. base
;; 65536 size 65536 — so the mapping the loader makes is the mapping the
;; theorems assume. The run-only examples/sha256sum/sha256sum_write.shard
;; write_file's this to examples/sha256sum/sha256sum_oneshot (re-homed
;; 2026-08-10, B6 slice D: the unqualified name went to the CPUID-dispatch
;; artifact under ruling R2, and this product's path now matches its bin name).
(fn shs_elf () (List Int) (enc_winelf (shs_elf_image) (entry_off) 65536 65536))
